    {
      "slug": "reddit-ticket-everything",
      "script": "Log everything? Our helpdesk manager had one rule: if it's not in the ticket system, it didn't happen — no ticket, no credit, no problem solved. So I logged everything. Every hallway question got a ticket. Every quick favor got a ticket. When he stopped me in the break room about the printer, I opened my laptop and filed a ticket right there: category Miscellaneous, priority Low. Administrative excellence. Three other techs copied me. Within a week, our team's ticket volume tripled — not because more broke, but because everything existed. His own director pulled the numbers and asked why one small team was drowning in requests nobody else logged. He couldn't blame us; this was literally his rule. The policy quietly vanished a month later. I still have four hundred tickets marked resolved. Who really broke the ticket system?",
      "editor_note": "Punched up to a three-word question hook, tightened the compliance escalation, added one dry administrative aside, and landed on the ticket-system reversal while preserving every fact, number, shot phrase, and query."
    },
    {
      "slug": "reddit-cart-cop-boxed-in",
      "script": "Cart law has consequences? A grocery-store guy policed the cart return like it was his personal mission. Leave a cart outside the corral, and he'd chase you down in the lot to lecture you about respect and laziness. He did this to me twice. The second time, his car was parked crooked across two spots, nose in a fire lane. When I caught him lecturing a stranger, I rounded up eleven stray carts and pushed every single one into the open space beside his car — correctly, one at a time, exactly how he wanted. Impeccable etiquette. When he noticed, his driver's door faced a wall of shopping carts four deep. He unloaded every cart himself in the rain, while the stranger he'd just lectured watched from her window. I said nothing. Which parking lesson did the cart cop remember?",
      "editor_note": "Punched up to a four-word question hook, compressed the parking-lot escalation, added one dry cart-etiquette aside, and landed on the cart cop's own parking lesson while preserving every fact, number, shot phrase, and query."
    }
